  "response": "## The finding\n\n```\ncrime_00039 · oversized_raster · \"Oversized Raster\"\nsrc/assets/hero-banner.png\nseverity: low · confidence: 0.95 · effort: quick\n```\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - `byte size: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)`\n> - `severity ladder: low ≥ 200 KB · medium ≥ 500 KB · high ≥ 1000 KB`\n> - `format: png`\n\nSo: **253.9 KB**, sitting just over the bottom rung of the ladder (200 KB), a quarter of the way to the `medium` threshold.\n\n## Verdict: request changes, don't block\n\nNothing here is broken or unsafe. `severity: low`, `agent_risk: 0.14` (the second-lowest in the whole report), `effort: quick` — this is hygiene, and a merge queue that blocks on hygiene stops being a signal people respect. Two things do argue for fixing it *before* merge rather than filing a follow-up:\n\n1. **It's the landing-page hero.** That makes it the LCP element on the highest-traffic route in the app. 253.9 KB of PNG on the critical path is a direct, measurable LCP cost, not a theoretical one — this is the one raster in the repo where the byte count converts straight into a Core Web Vitals number.\n2. **The fix is quick and self-contained.** No build change, no code change, no reviewer re-reading logic. Swapping the asset is cheaper than the round-trip of a tracking ticket.\n\nSo: **request changes** — one round, \"re-encode and resize the asset, then re-push.\" If the PR is time-pressured (a launch, a demo), merging and fixing in a same-day follow-up is defensible; this is not a hill to block a release on.\n\n**One caveat on the evidence.** The finding reports byte size but *not* pixel dimensions — compare `crime_00038`, where the detector does report `32 × 32 px`. So crimes has told you the asset is heavy; it has *not* told you whether it's heavy because it's over-dimensioned, under-compressed, or both. Check the intrinsic size before picking a target (DevTools → hover the `<img>` → \"Intrinsic size\" vs \"Rendered size\"). If it's a 3000px-wide PNG rendering into a 1440px box, the resize is most of your win; if it's already 1440px wide, this is purely a format problem.\n\n## The transformation\n\n**Target dimensions.** Size to the rendered box, then cap at 2× for retina:\n\n| Breakpoint | Rendered box (CSS px) | Ship |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mobile | ~390 wide | 780w |\n| Tablet | ~768 wide | 1536w |\n| Desktop | ~1440 wide | 2560w (capped) |\n\nEmit those three widths behind `srcset` with an accurate `sizes` (`sizes=\"100vw\"` for a full-bleed hero) rather than one asset for everyone. The 2560w cap is deliberate: beyond ~2560px the returns on a photographic hero are invisible on real displays and the bytes are not. If the design is a fixed-width hero rather than full-bleed, substitute the actual max-width — the rule is *2× the largest CSS box it ever occupies*, not 2× the design canvas.\n\n**Budget:** the desktop variant should land **under 100 KB**, and the mobile variant under 30 KB. If a 2560w AVIF won't fit 100 KB, the image has more detail than a hero needs — that's a signal to crop or simplify, not to raise the budget.\n\n**Format: AVIF as the primary, WebP as the fallback**, via `<picture>` (or whatever your framework's image component emits — most do this automatically and you should use it rather than hand-rolling the markup).\n\nWhy AVIF first, for *this* asset specifically:\n\n- On large photographic images with smooth gradients — exactly what a hero banner is — AVIF typically lands **20–50% smaller than WebP** at visually equivalent quality. That gap widens as the image gets bigger, so the hero is where it pays most.\n- AVIF's chroma handling and higher internal bit depth avoid the gradient banding WebP's 8-bit 4:2:0 path shows on skies, soft backgrounds, and brand gradients. Heroes are full of those.\n- Support is now broadly baseline (Chrome, Firefox, Safari 16+, Edge), so the WebP `<source>` is a genuine fallback, not the path most users take.\n\nWhy WebP still earns its place as the second `<source>`: universal support, and meaningfully **faster decode on low-end mobile**. That decode cost is the one real argument against AVIF for an LCP element — on a cheap Android, a large AVIF can spend back some of the transfer time it saved. The `<picture>` ordering gets you the small bytes where AVIF is supported and a fast, safe path everywhere else.\n\n**If your pipeline can only emit one format, pick WebP** — the universal-support-plus-fast-decode combination is the safer single bet for an LCP image, and it still gets you most of the way down from 253.9 KB. But two `<source>` entries is a few lines of markup, so the single-format constraint is usually worth removing rather than working around.\n\nConcretely:\n\n```bash\n# desktop, AVIF primary\nnpx @squoosh/cli --avif '{\"cqLevel\":30}' --resize '{\"width\":2560}' src/assets/hero-banner.png\n# desktop, WebP fallback\nnpx @squoosh/cli --webp '{\"quality\":78}' --resize '{\"width\":2560}' src/assets/hero-banner.png\n```\n\nTune `cqLevel` (lower = higher quality) until you hit the budget, then eyeball the gradients at full size before accepting it.\n\n## Worth adding while you're here\n\nPut a **size budget in CI** so the next hero doesn't reintroduce this — either a `crimes scan` gate on `oversized_raster`, or a bundle-size check on `src/assets/`. The detector already knows the thresholds; wiring it into the PR check turns this from a thing reviewers have to notice into a thing that can't land.\n\n## Not this PR's problem\n\nTwo other asset findings are in the report but **pre-existing** — don't attach them to this review:\n\n- `crime_00029` — `src/assets/partner-logo.svg` has two base64 rasters embedded in it (`severity: high`, 0.95 confidence). This is the more serious asset defect in the repo and deserves its own issue.\n- `crime_00038` — `src/assets/check-icon.png` is a 32×32 PNG that should be an SVG (`severity: low`).\n\nFlag them to the author as follow-ups if they're touching assets anyway, but the hero PR shouldn't grow to absorb them.",
